<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <%- include('partials/head') %>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/dashboard.css">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/watch-yourself.css">
</head>
<body class="app-body">
  <div class="app-layout">
    <%- include('partials/sidebar') %>
    <main class="main-content">

      <a href="/resources" class="wy-back">← Back to Resources</a>

      <article class="wy-article">

        <!-- ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <header class="wy-hero">
          <img class="wy-hero-art" src="/images/greenhouse/wateringcan-reflection.webp"
               alt="" width="260" height="177">
          <h1 class="wy-title">Watch Yourself: A Guide</h1>
          <p class="wy-frame">Turning Your Inner Critic Into a Helpful Gardener</p>
        </header>

        <!-- ── 1. Meet your critic ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">Meet your critic</h2>
          <p>
            When you sit down to watch your own recordings, a voice usually
            shows up. You know the one.
          </p>
          <ul class="wy-quotes">
            <li>you look weird.</li>
            <li>why did you say that.</li>
            <li>nobody wants to hear this.</li>
            <li>your face does a thing.</li>
            <li>why do you talk like that.</li>
            <li>this is boring.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            This voice isn't lying to hurt you. It's trying, in its own
            clumsy way, to protect you from something — embarrassment,
            dismissal, exposure. It thinks the job is to prune everything
            that could hurt you before anyone else can.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trouble is: it prunes too fast. It prunes anything green.
            It composts things that were just starting to root.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 2. What a gardener does instead ──────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">What a gardener does instead</h2>
          <p>
            A gardener has the same eye. Same attention. Same instinct for
            cutting away what's not thriving.
          </p>
          <p>
            But a gardener is patient. A gardener asks <em>why</em> before
            <em>out</em>. A gardener knows the difference between a weed
            and a wildflower that just hasn't bloomed yet.
          </p>
          <p class="wy-callout">Same voice. Different job.</p>
          <div class="wy-reframe-list">
            <div class="wy-reframe">
              <p class="wy-reframe-critic">you look weird.</p>
              <p class="wy-reframe-gardener">
                → there's something honest in her face here. what is she
                trying to say?
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-reframe">
              <p class="wy-reframe-critic">why did you say that.</p>
              <p class="wy-reframe-gardener">
                → what were you circling around? there might be something
                worth naming underneath.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-reframe">
              <p class="wy-reframe-critic">nobody wants to hear this.</p>
              <p class="wy-reframe-gardener">
                → not yet, maybe. but who <em>might</em>, if this were
                shaped a little?
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-reframe">
              <p class="wy-reframe-critic">this is boring.</p>
              <p class="wy-reframe-gardener">
                → what makes it feel flat? is there a moment inside it
                that isn't?
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Notice the shift. The critic passes verdicts. The gardener asks
            questions. The critic makes a pile. The gardener tends the
            whole plot.
          </p>
          <p>
            Your job in Spring is to teach the critic the gardener's
            questions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 3. Keeping the gardener in the garden ────────────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">
            Keep the gardener in the garden — not in the recording booth
          </h2>
          <p>
            The recording process — Winter — needs to be a garden with the
            gate closed. No critic. No gardener. Just planting.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the gardener shows up while you're recording, it will start
            pruning seeds. It will kill a thought before it's a sentence.
            It will make you rerecord until you sound like someone you're
            not. It will trade honesty for polish and call it improvement.
          </p>
          <p class="wy-callout">
            Recording is for planting.<br>
            Watching is for tending.<br>
            Two different modes. Two different seasons. Keep them apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you notice the critic-turning-gardener showing up in your
            daily recording, gently walk it back out. Tell it: not now.
            There's a whole season for you. Come back then.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 4. Practical tips ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">Practical tips for when watching is hard</h2>
          <p>
            Watching yourself back is a skill. Most people find it
            uncomfortable the first fifty times. Here are permissions and
            workarounds:
          </p>
          <div class="wy-tips">
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">Watch on mute first.</h3>
              <p>
                Read what your body is saying without the pressure of your
                own voice. You'll be surprised how much lands.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">Then watch at 1.25×.</h3>
              <p>
                Faster playback makes the delivery feel less precious and
                lets the ideas surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">Angle doesn't matter.</h3>
              <p>
                Bad lighting doesn't matter. Bad hair doesn't matter. You
                are not evaluating a broadcast. You are visiting a seedling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">Take notes about ideas, not looks.</h3>
              <p>
                Every time you catch yourself narrating your face,
                redirect: <em>what am I saying? what am I trying to say?</em>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">Watch twice before deciding.</h3>
              <p>
                First pass to feel it. Second pass to hear it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="wy-tip">
              <h3 class="wy-tip-title">If you flinch, skip forward.</h3>
              <p>
                You can come back. Some cuttings need a few weeks of
                distance before they're readable.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 5. Ways to find use in all sorts of content ──────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">Ways to find use in all sorts of content</h2>
          <p>
            Once you find something worth tending, don't limit yourself to
            the format you recorded in. The same idea can become many
            things:
          </p>
          <ul class="wy-formats">
            <li>
              <strong>Voice only.</strong>
              Extract the audio, add a still or a quote card. Great when
              you love what you said but not how you look saying it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Turn it into a carousel.</strong>
              Write it out as a post. Break the idea into slides. Same
              thought, no camera.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Text over the video.</strong>
              Add captions or a headline; play with sound off or with
              reduced pressure on delivery.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A written essay.</strong>
              Some of your best videos are essays in disguise. Let them
              become that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A snippet inside a longer piece.</strong>
              One good line can anchor a whole post.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A conversation starter.</strong>
              Post it as a question and invite others in.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="wy-callout">A cutting is a seed, not a shape. The shape is up to you.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- ── 6. FAQ ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="wy-section">
          <h2 class="wy-section-title">FAQ</h2>

          <div class="wy-faq">
            <h3 class="wy-faq-q">What if the critic is too loud?</h3>
            <p>
              Close the laptop. Take a walk. Come back tomorrow. This work
              is not going anywhere. Watching yourself when you're already
              dysregulated will just teach the critic that this is
              dangerous — and it'll show up louder next time.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="wy-faq">
            <h3 class="wy-faq-q">What if nothing feels usable?</h3>
            <p>
              Usually this means one of three things: (1) you're watching
              too soon and the recording is still too raw — give it a
              week; (2) you're in critic mode, not gardener mode — reread
              the top of this page; or (3) you've had a season of
              recording that was more processing than creating, and the
              tending will feel lean this time. All three are fine. Some
              seasons are for growing. Some are for composting.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="wy-faq">
            <h3 class="wy-faq-q">What if I'm just bored watching myself?</h3>
            <p>
              Bored is information. Bored can mean the idea isn't fully
              yours yet — you're circling something you already know, or
              something you're just repeating. Bored can also mean the
              idea <em>is</em> yours but the delivery is flat. If it's the
              first, compost it. If it's the second, cultivate it — the
              idea is worth reshaping.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="wy-faq">
            <h3 class="wy-faq-q">Should I edit out my filler words?</h3>
            <p>
              Not while tending. That's a Summer question. Right now
              you're deciding what has life. How you eventually deliver it
              is a separate step.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="wy-faq">
            <h3 class="wy-faq-q">Does this apply to people who look and sound polished naturally?</h3>
            <p>
              Yes. Polish is not the same as depth. Some of the most
              polished creators are the most stuck — they've optimized
              their delivery so much they no longer say things that
              surprise themselves. The gardener's questions are as useful
              for them. Probably more.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <p class="wy-signoff">
          You planted these thoughts.<br>
          Now come see what they became.
        </p>

      </article>

    </main>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
